.TH MAIL_FILTER 8 "August 2026" "mail_filter 1.0.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mail_filter \- MIME-aware Subject and body milter for Postfix
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mail_filter
.br
.B mail_filter
.B \-\-configtest
.br
.B mail_filter
.B \-\-test
.RI [ test-options ]
.br
.B mail_filter
.B \-\-test\-eml
.I message.eml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mail_filter
is a Postfix milter daemon that accepts, rejects, or warns on incoming mail
according to user-defined regular-expression rules. Rules can inspect the
decoded Subject, the raw message body, SMTP envelope sender and recipients,
and arbitrary message headers \(em in any combination, within a single rule.
.PP
.B mail_filter
runs as a single process registering one Unix-domain socket with Postfix,
and evaluates one rules file that can freely combine
.B subject
and
.B body
conditions in the same rule.
.PP
Before rules are evaluated, the Subject and message-header values are
unfolded and decoded from RFC\ 2047 MIME encoded-words (both Q-encoding and
Base64). This means regex patterns for Subject, From, To, Cc, Reply-To, and
arbitrary header tests are matched against human-readable Unicode text rather
than the raw encoded wire representation. SMTP envelope addresses are not MIME
headers and are therefore used unchanged. The message body is matched as raw
UTF-8 text (not MIME- or HTML-decoded); see
.B Body Handling
below.
.PP
Connecting clients whose IP addresses are listed in the whitelist file
bypass rule evaluation entirely; their messages are always accepted.
.PP
The milter communicates with Postfix over a Unix domain socket and,
when a matching REJECT rule is found, replies with a permanent
550\ 5.7.1 failure code so the sending server receives a definitive
rejection rather than a bounce.
.SH OPERATION
.SS MIME Decoding
Subject and message-header values delivered as encoded-words are decoded before matching.
Both
.I Q-encoding
.RI ( =?UTF-8?Q?...?= )
and
.I Base64-encoding
.RI ( =?UTF-8?B?...?= )
are handled for any charset advertised in the encoded-word.
If a charset is unknown the bytes are decoded as Latin-1 with
replacement characters for unmappable sequences.
If the header contains no encoded-words it is used as-is.
.PP
The Subject retains its historical raw and decoded logging fields. Common
address headers are logged in decoded, unfolded form; the optional structured
mail log also provides explicit raw variants for From, To, Cc, and Reply-To.
.SS Application Log Timestamps
The timestamp written to the ordinary mail_filter application log is
controlled by the
.B log_timestamp
setting in
.BR mail_filter.conf (5).
The default is
.B bsd
on FreeBSD and
.B iso8601
on Linux/Debian, matching each platform's native syslog convention.
.B bsd
is the historical format, for example
.I Aug 28 18:50:23 .
.B iso8601
is an offset-aware local timestamp with microsecond precision, for
example
.IR 2026-08-28T18:50:23.652662+00:00 .
Any other value is used as a format string for Python's
.IR time.strftime() ,
letting an administrator match a house log style; see
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
for the full set of caveats (notably that
.I %f
is not supported).
.PP
This setting applies only to the file written directly by mail_filter,
typically
.IR /var/log/mail_filter.log .
It is independent of the optional structured
.B [mail_log]
output. The latter is sent through syslog(3), so its timestamp format is
determined by the host's syslog implementation. mail_filter deliberately
does not try to detect or duplicate rsyslog, syslog-ng, or syslogd timestamp
templates automatically.
.SS Rule Evaluation
Rules are read from the rules file and evaluated in order.
Conditions may test the decoded Subject, the raw message body, envelope
addresses, and decoded, unfolded message headers, combined with
.BR and , " " or ", and " not
(or the legacy
.BR anyof / allof / not
function forms). Each regex test may use an inline
.I /regex/flags
literal or a
.B refile:
reference to a file of regex literals.
Evaluation stops at a terminal
.B reject
or
.B accept
action;
.B warn
and
.B dunno
allow evaluation to continue. If no terminal rule matches, the message
is accepted.
See
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5)
for the full rule syntax, including the
.B body
test.
.SS Body Handling
The milter accumulates the message body as it streams in, up to the
.B max_body_kb
limit configured in
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
(default: 512\ KB). Body data received beyond that limit is discarded;
.B body
rules still run against the retained (truncated) prefix, and the
.B body_truncated
mail-log field records whether truncation occurred.
.PP
The retained body is decoded as UTF-8 (with replacement characters for
invalid sequences) but is otherwise used as-is: it is
.B not
stripped of HTML tags, not MIME-decoded part-by-part, and not
otherwise normalized, except for one transformation \(em quoted-printable
soft line breaks
.RI ( "=\en" " or " "=\er\en" )
are merged first, so that a pattern such as an HTML entity artificially
split across a QP line boundary
.RI ( "&#22=\er\en5;" )
is rejoined
.RI ( "&#225;" )
before matching. This specifically targets a common spam-obfuscation
technique of encoding body text as HTML entities and injecting soft
line breaks to defeat naive pattern matching; see the
.B entity_count
mail-log field and
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5)
for a ready-made detection rule.
.PP
When the message has no body at all (or the milter cannot decode it),
.B body
tests simply see an empty string and no
.B body
rule matches \(em rules that test only
.BR subject ,
.BR envelope_from ,
or other non-body conditions are still evaluated normally.
.SS Whitelisting
When a connecting client's IP address matches an entry in the whitelist
the milter accepts the message without evaluating any rules \(em this
applies to
.B all
rules, whether they test the Subject, the body, or both.
The ACCEPT decision is logged at INFO level together with the SMTP envelope
addresses, the available From:, To:, X-Original-To:, and Delivered-To:
header values, and the retained body size and entity count, which allows
the whitelist to serve as a passive audit trail for trusted senders.
See
.BR mail_filter_whitelist.cidr (5)
for the file format.
.SS Configuration file paths
The file-valued settings
.BR rules ,
.B whitelist
and
.B logfile
may be written as absolute paths or as paths relative to the directory
containing
.BR mail_filter.conf .
For example, when the rules file is stored beside the configuration file,
.PP
.nf
.RS
rules = mail_filter_rules.conf
.RE
.fi
.PP
is sufficient; a full path is not required. Likewise, relative
.B refile:
paths in the rules file are resolved relative to the rules file's directory.
This makes a complete alternate configuration tree portable as a directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-configtest
Validate the configuration, rules, and whitelist files, then exit.
A structured report is written to standard output.
Exit status is 0 if all checks pass, or a positive integer equal to
the number of errors found.
This option is equivalent to
.B service mail_filter configtest
on FreeBSD or
.B mail_filter \-\-configtest
run manually on Linux.
If any
.B \-\-test*
or Subject/body test switch (below) is also given,
.B \-\-configtest
still runs alone; a
.B WARN
line is printed to standard error naming the ignored switches.
.TP
.B \-\-test
Test a supplied message context against the currently configured rules.
The matched rule path is printed. For every positive regex match the output
shows the exact regex literal; a
.B refile:
match additionally shows the referenced file and line number.
Exit status is 0 for ACCEPT and 1 for REJECT.
.TP
.BI \-\-test\-config " file"
Use an alternative
.B mail_filter.conf
(and the rules/whitelist it references) for
.BR \-\-test ", " \-\-test\-eml ", and " \-\-configtest .
.TP
.BI \-\-test\-eml " file"
Test a complete RFC\ 2822/mbox message file: the Subject, From, To, Date,
and body are extracted automatically (QP soft-breaks merged, MIME parts
listed), and the rules are evaluated against them.
Exit status is 0 for ACCEPT and 1 for REJECT.
If
.BR \-\-test\-context ,
.BR \-\-subject ,
.BR \-\-body ,
or any other Subject/body test switch below is also given, a
.B WARN
is printed to standard error and those switches are ignored \(em
.B \-\-test\-eml
always builds its own context from the EML file.
.TP
.BI \-\-test\-context " file"
Read the full test message context \(em Subject, body, envelope, and
headers together \(em from a JSON file; see
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5)
for the JSON schema.
If any Subject/envelope/header or body switch below is also given
alongside
.BR \-\-test\-context ,
a
.B WARN
is printed to standard error and those switches are ignored;
.B \-\-test\-context
takes precedence.
.PP
The remaining test switches are split into two groups that are meant to
be
.B combined
in a single
.B \-\-test
invocation \(em this is how a rule that requires both a Subject
.I and
a body condition (e.g. a Business-Email-Compromise style rule) is
exercised from the command line. They are documentation groups, not a
restriction: unlike
.BR \-\-configtest / \-\-test\-eml / \-\-test\-context " above, mixing "
Subject-side and body-side switches is the normal, supported case and
produces no warning.
.SS Subject / envelope / header test options
.TP
.BI \-\-subject " subject-string"
Set the raw Subject header. RFC 2047 encoded-words are decoded before rule
evaluation.
.TP
.BI \-\-envelope\-from " address"
Set the SMTP MAIL FROM value.
.TP
.BI \-\-envelope\-to " address"
Add an SMTP RCPT TO value. This option may be specified more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " value"
Set a From: header value. It is unfolded and RFC 2047 decoded before matching.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " value"
Set a To: header value. It is unfolded and RFC 2047 decoded before matching.
.TP
.BI \-\-header " Name:Value"
Add an arbitrary message header. This option may be specified more than once. Header values are unfolded and RFC 2047 decoded before matching.
.SS Body test options
.TP
.BI \-\-body " text"
Set the message body directly from the command line.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-body\-file ;
giving both is a hard argument-parsing error, not a warning.
.TP
.BI \-\-body\-file " file"
Read the message body from a file.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-body ,
as above.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Subject-only test
mail_filter \-\-test \-\-subject 'urgent payment' \-\-envelope\-from '<sender@example.com>' \-\-header 'X-Spam-Status: Yes'

# Body-only test
mail_filter \-\-test \-\-body 'please wire the funds to our new account'

# Combined Subject + body test (e.g. a BEC-style rule)
mail_filter \-\-test \-\-subject 'urgent payment' \-\-body 'please wire the funds' \-\-envelope\-from '<ceo@external.example.com>'

# Whole message file
mail_filter \-\-test\-eml /tmp/suspect-message.eml
.RE
.fi
.SH POSTFIX INTEGRATION
Add the socket path to
.IR /etc/postfix/main.cf
(Linux) or
.IR /usr/local/etc/postfix/main.cf
(FreeBSD):
.PP
.nf
.RS
smtpd_milters        = unix:private/mail_filter.sock
non_smtpd_milters    = unix:private/mail_filter.sock
milter_default_action = accept
milter_protocol       = 6
.RE
.fi
.PP
The path in
.B smtpd_milters
is interpreted relative to the Postfix queue directory.
On Linux Postfix typically runs inside a chroot at
.IR /var/spool/postfix ,
so the socket must reside under that directory tree.
The default installation places it at
.IR /var/spool/postfix/private/mail_filter.sock ,
which is referenced as
.IR unix:private/mail_filter.sock .
.PP
On FreeBSD Postfix does not chroot by default.
The socket is at
.I /var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.sock
and must be referenced using a full absolute path:
.PP
.nf
.RS
smtpd_milters = unix:/var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.sock
.RE
.fi
.PP
If multiple milters are already configured, append the socket path to
the existing value with a space or comma separator.
After modifying
.I main.cf
reload Postfix:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# FreeBSD
service postfix reload
# Linux
postfix reload
.RE
.fi
.PP
The
.B milter_default_action = accept
setting ensures that mail is delivered normally if the milter is
temporarily unavailable.
Change to
.B tempfail
if you prefer mail to be deferred when the milter cannot be reached.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reload the rules file and whitelist file.
In-flight connections are not interrupted; the new configuration takes
effect for all subsequent connections.
This is the standard way to deploy rule changes without a service
restart.
On FreeBSD:
.BR "service mail_filter reload" .
On Linux:
.BR "systemctl reload mail_filter" .
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Graceful shutdown.
A final log message is written and the process exits immediately via
.IR _exit (2),
terminating all milter threads.
The Unix socket file is cleaned up by the rc.d stop handler (FreeBSD)
or the systemd unit (Linux) after the process exits.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
.B Do not send SIGHUP to the milter.
The underlying
.I libmilter
C library intercepts SIGHUP at the C level and terminates the process
without invoking the Python signal handler.
Use
.B SIGUSR1
to reload configuration, not SIGHUP.
.SH FREEBSD RC.D INTEGRATION
The rc.d script
.I /usr/local/etc/rc.d/mail_filter
provides the following commands:
.TP 24
.B start
Start the milter via
.IR daemon (8).
The socket directory is created or repaired, any stale socket file is
removed, and the log file is created if absent.
After forking,
.BR daemon (8)
writes the PID of the Python process to
.IR /var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.pid .
The start command waits up to five seconds and verifies that the
process is still alive.
.TP
.B stop
Send
.B SIGTERM
and remove the socket file.
.TP
.B restart
Stop, then start.
.TP
.B reload
Send
.B SIGUSR1
to reload rules and whitelist without a restart.
.TP
.B configtest
Run a comprehensive sanity check: verify the Python binary, check the
script for syntax errors, and validate every rule and whitelist entry.
Exits 0 on success.
.PP
To enable at boot add to
.IR /etc/rc.conf :
.PP
.nf
.RS
mail_filter_enable="YES"
.RE
.fi
.PP
The rc.d script honours the following
.I rc.conf
variables (defaults shown):
.TP 36
.B mail_filter_runas=postfix
User account under which the Python process runs.
.TP
.B mail_filter_runas_group=postfix
Group for the Python process.
.TP
.B mail_filter_sockdir=/var/run/mail_filter
Directory that holds the Unix socket and PID file.
.TP
.B mail_filter_python=/usr/local/bin/python3
Full path to the Python 3 interpreter.
Change this if a different Python version is installed.
.TP
.B mail_filter_script=/usr/local/lib/mail_filter/mail_filter.py
Full path to the milter script. Not
.IR /usr/local/sbin/mail_filter ;
that is the thin wrapper admins run interactively, not the real
implementation rc.d execs directly (see FILES below).
.TP
.B mail_filter_config=/usr/local/etc/mail_filter/mail_filter.conf
Full path to the main configuration file.
.SH LINUX SYSTEMD INTEGRATION
The systemd unit
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/mail_filter.service
runs the milter as user and group
.IR postfix .
Before the process starts, systemd compiles the Python script to
detect syntax errors (ExecStartPre).
If the milter exits unexpectedly it is automatically restarted after
five seconds (Restart=on-failure).
.PP
Common management commands:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl start   mail_filter
systemctl stop    mail_filter
systemctl restart mail_filter
systemctl reload  mail_filter   # sends SIGUSR1, reloads rules
systemctl status  mail_filter
journalctl \-u mail_filter \-f  # follow journal
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Note:
The milter writes to its own log file (see
.B FILES
below) rather than the journal, so
.BR journalctl (1)
only shows startup and shutdown messages emitted directly by systemd.
.SH LOGGING
Log output goes to the file configured by the
.I logfile
key in
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
(default:
.IR /var/log/mail_filter.log ).
Each final message decision is written as one physical audit-log line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
Mon DD HH:MM:SS mail_filter[PID] LEVEL [ID] field=value ...
.RE
.fi
.PP
The line contains the envelope addresses, relevant message headers, raw and
decoded Subject, final result, and matching-rule details when applicable.
Embedded newlines and other unusual characters in logged values are escaped
by Python representation formatting, so they do not split the audit record.
When DEBUG logging is enabled, protocol-level diagnostic messages such as
CONNECT, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, and individual headers are intentionally emitted
as additional lines; the final message decision remains a single line.
.PP
.I ID
is a per-connection integer.
Common fields:
.TP 16
.B qid
Postfix queue ID.
.TP
.B msgid
Value of the Message-ID header.
.TP
.B ip
Client IP address.
.TP
.B envelope_from
SMTP envelope sender (MAIL FROM). This is distinct from the message's
From: header.
.TP
.B envelope_to
SMTP envelope recipients (RCPT TO), comma-separated.
.TP
.B header_from
Value of the message From: header, or a dash if the header is absent.
.TP
.B header_to
Value of the message To: header, or a dash if the header is absent.
.TP
.B x_original_to
Value of the X-Original-To: header, or a dash if the header is absent.
This is logged separately because some delivered messages do not contain
a To: header.
.TP
.B delivered_to
Value of the Delivered-To: header, or a dash if the header is absent.
Multiple occurrences of any logged address header are separated by
`` | ``.
.TP
.B subject_raw
Verbatim Subject header value.
.TP
.B subject_decoded
Subject after MIME encoded-word decoding.
.TP
.B decode_error
Subject decoding error text, or a dash when decoding succeeded.
.TP
.B body_size
Number of bytes of message body retained for filtering, after any
.B max_body_kb
truncation.
.TP
.B body_truncated
.B yes
if the body was cut off at the configured
.B max_body_kb
limit,
.B no
otherwise.
.TP
.B entity_count
Count of HTML-entity-style sequences found in the retained body after
QP soft-break merging. See
.B Body Handling
above.
.TP
.B result
Final message decision, normally ACCEPT or REJECT.
.TP
.B reason
Reason for an ACCEPT result when applicable, for example no_rule_matched, rule,
whitelisted, or fallthrough.
.TP
.B warn
Triggered warning message or messages, or a dash. Multiple warnings are joined
with `` | ``.
.TP
.B warn_rules
Rule-path and regex-match details associated with warnings, or a dash.
.TP
.B rule_line
Line-number path through the matching rule and any nested rules, ending
at the action line.
.TP
.B matched
Semicolon-separated regex match details. Inline regexes identify the rules
file and line. A
.B refile:
match identifies the referenced regex file, its line number, and the exact
.I /pattern/flags
literal. A dash means the successful path contained no positive regex
match, for example a true negated condition or an else branch.
.PP
Log levels:
.TP 12
.B DEBUG
Every connection, every header, every rule comparison, all internal
state transitions.
Produces high log volume; use only for troubleshooting.
.TP
.B INFO
Every accepted and rejected message with full context (IP, addresses,
both Subject forms, body size, entity count).
Suitable for production audit logging.
.TP
.B WARNING
Rejected messages, WARN rule hits, reload events, and startup/shutdown
notices.
Suitable when disk space or log volume is a concern.
.TP
.B ERROR
Serious errors only (files not found, socket directory problems).
.PP
The log level can be changed in
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
and takes effect after a service restart.
Changing it does not require reloading the rules.
.PP
On FreeBSD log rotation is configured by
.IR /etc/newsyslog.conf.d/mail_filter.conf .
On Linux log rotation is configured by
.IR /etc/logrotate.d/mail_filter .
The milter uses a
.I WatchedFileHandler
that detects log rotation automatically and reopens the file without
requiring a signal or restart.
.SH STRUCTURED MAIL LOG
mail_filter can emit one configurable message record through
.BR syslog (3)
using the
.B mail
facility. The feature is configured in the
.B [mail_log]
section of
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
and is disabled by default.
.PP
This output is separate from
.IR /var/log/mail_filter.log .
Its purpose is to place selected metadata already decoded by mail_filter
next to the normal Postfix records in the system mail log. No second milter is
required.
.PP
A typical backup-MX configuration is:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[mail_log]
enabled  = yes
priority = warning
tag      = postfix/mail_filter
format   = {qid}:\\theader Subject: '{subject_decoded}'\\teFrom: '{envelope_from}'\\thFrom: '{header_from}'\\tbody_size={body_size_bytes}\\tentity_count={entity_count}
.RE
.fi
.PP
On Ubuntu this normally produces records in
.IR /var/log/mail.log .
On FreeBSD the normal destination is
.IR /var/log/maillog .
Local syslog configuration ultimately determines the destination.
.PP
The template can use queue ID, Message-ID, client IP, envelope addresses,
selected address headers, raw and decoded Subject, decoding status, final
milter result, warning information, and matching-rule details. See
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
for the complete field list and escaping rules.
.PP
.B --configtest
validates every field reference before the service is started.


.SH CONTENT-FILTER REINJECTION AND MULTIPLE RECORDS
The structured
.B [mail_log]
output is emitted once for every completed mail_filter transaction. If
Postfix passes a message through an external content filter and the filter
reinserts the message into Postfix, the same logical message can pass through
mail_filter twice.
.PP
A common SpamAssassin configuration is:
.PP
.nf
.RS
smtp      inet  n       -       y       -       -       smtpd
  -o content_filter=spamassassin

spamassassin unix -     n       n       -       -       pipe
  user=debian-spamd argv=/usr/bin/spamc -f -e
  /usr/sbin/sendmail -oi -f ${sender} ${recipient}
.RE
.fi
.PP
The SMTP pass receives the original message and has one Postfix queue ID.
.B spamc
then returns the filtered message through the local
.B sendmail
interface. Postfix accepts that returned message as a new queue entry, assigns
a new queue ID, and invokes the milter again. The result is two mail_filter
mail-log records, usually with the same Message-ID but different queue IDs.
This is an expected property of the Postfix content-filter/reinjection design,
not a mail_filter logging defect.
.PP
The two passes can also differ in envelope formatting or headers because the
second pass is a new Postfix submission. mail_filter deliberately logs the
values observed in each individual transaction.
.PP
On Ubuntu, if TAB separators configured with
.B \\t
appear as
.B #011
in
.IR /var/log/mail.log ,
see
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
for the required rsyslog setting
.BR global(parser.escapeControlCharacterTab="off") .

.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B Milter not reachable \(em mail deferred or accepted without filtering
Verify the socket path.
On Linux, confirm the socket is inside the Postfix queue directory
and that it exists:
.nf
.RS

ls \-la /var/spool/postfix/private/mail_filter.sock
.RE
.fi
On FreeBSD:
.nf
.RS

ls \-la /var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.sock
.RE
.fi
If the socket is missing, check whether the milter is running and
review the log file.
.TP
.B Rule not matching an expected subject
Use
.B \-\-test
to decode the Subject and trace rule evaluation:
.nf
.RS

mail_filter \-\-test \-\-subject 'the subject line here'
.RE
.fi
Pass the raw wire form of the Subject header (with
.I =?...?=
encoded-words) to test encoding-sensitive patterns.
.TP
.B Rule not matching an expected body pattern
Use
.B \-\-test
with
.B \-\-body
(or
.BR \-\-body\-file / \-\-test\-eml )
to trace body rule evaluation, and check the printed
.B body_preview
and
.B entity_count
to confirm the milter received and decoded the body you expect:
.nf
.RS

mail_filter \-\-test \-\-body 'the body text here'
.RE
.fi
If the body is longer than the configured
.BR max_body_kb ,
only the retained (truncated) prefix is available to rules; increase
.B max_body_kb
in
.BR mail_filter.conf (5)
if legitimate matches are being missed near the end of long messages.
.TP
.B Regex compilation error at startup
Run
.B \-\-configtest
to identify the offending line number in the rules file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/mail_filter\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /usr/sbin/mail_filter\ \ (Linux)
The milter command admins actually run. On both platforms this is a
thin wrapper; the real implementation lives in
.IR /usr/local/lib/mail_filter/ " (FreeBSD) or " /usr/lib/mail_filter/ " (Linux)."
FreeBSD keeps this under
.I /usr/local
because that is pkg(8)'s own native prefix
.RI ( hier (7):
.I /usr/local/lib
is for "local libraries",
.I /usr/local/sbin
for "local administration utilities" \(em the launcher is a utility,
its importable
.B mail_filter
package next to it is a library, not itself a utility; real precedent
for the same split already on this machine: dovecot ships under
.IR /usr/local/lib/dovecot/ ,
not
.IR /usr/local/sbin/ ).
Linux keeps it entirely outside
.I /usr/local
instead (Debian Policy 9.1.1 reserves that hierarchy for the system
administrator's own installs, not package-owned files), landing at
.I /usr/lib/mail_filter/
to match how Postfix itself ships its own internal daemons on Debian
.RI ( /usr/lib/postfix/sbin/ ) .
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/mail_filter.conf\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /etc/mail_filter/mail_filter.conf\ \ (Linux)
Main configuration file, alongside
.B mail_filter_rules.conf
and
.BR mail_filter_whitelist.cidr .
On Linux, the whole
.I /etc/mail_filter
directory (these files are never dpkg conffiles, see
.BR mail_filter.conf (5))
is deleted by
.B apt purge
but left untouched by
.BR "apt remove" .
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/mail_filter_rules.conf\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /etc/mail_filter/mail_filter_rules.conf\ \ (Linux)
Filter rules.
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/mail_filter_whitelist.cidr\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /etc/mail_filter/mail_filter_whitelist.cidr\ \ (Linux)
IP/CIDR whitelist.
.TP
.I /var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.sock\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /var/spool/postfix/private/mail_filter.sock\ \ (Linux)
Unix domain socket.
.TP
.I /var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.pid\ \ (FreeBSD)
PID file written by
.IR daemon (8).
.TP
.I /var/log/mail_filter.log
Log file, plus any rotated
.I mail_filter.log.*
siblings (see below). On Linux, deleted by
.B apt purge
but left untouched by
.BR "apt remove" .
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/rc.d/mail_filter\ \ (FreeBSD)
rc.d service script.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/mail_filter.service\ \ (Linux)
systemd unit file.
.TP
.I /etc/newsyslog.conf.d/mail_filter.conf\ \ (FreeBSD)
newsyslog log rotation configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/logrotate.d/mail_filter\ \ (Linux)
logrotate log rotation configuration.
